{
  "id": "bwxoetn",
  "title": "RRB PHYSICS – CHAPTER 7",
  "order": 40,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "h2uzvqj",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "LIGHT"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Light exhibits dual nature and undergoes reflection, refraction, dispersion, total internal reflection, and scattering.",
        "The Mirror Formula is given by 1/f = 1/v + 1/u, while the Lens Formula is 1/f = 1/v - 1/u.",
        "Power of a lens is the reciprocal of its focal length in meters (P = 1/f) and is expressed in Dioptres (D).",
        "Total Internal Reflection requires light to travel from a denser to a rarer medium at an angle of incidence greater than the critical angle.",
        "Defects of vision like Myopia are corrected with concave lenses, whereas Hypermetropia is corrected with convex lenses."
      ],
      "id": "fz64q98"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Chapter Topics",
          "value": "21 Sections",
          "icon": "book"
        },
        {
          "label": "Core Subject",
          "value": "Optics & Light",
          "icon": "award"
        },
        {
          "label": "Key Formulae",
          "value": "Mirror & Lens Rules",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
          "label": "Exam Relevance",
          "value": "High Yield RRB Topic",
          "icon": "info"
        }
      ],
      "id": "zsyoujf"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "owzshfu",
      "content": "Light is a form of energy that enables us to see objects. It travels in the form of electromagnetic waves and does not require a medium."
    },
    {
      "id": "sn1hi70",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "1. NATURE OF LIGHT"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "b83jx9s",
      "content": "• Light is an electromagnetic wave."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3leovs7",
      "content": "• It travels with speed 3 × 10⁸ m/s in vacuum."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nv8v6n9",
      "content": "• It shows both wave and particle nature (dual nature)."
    },
    {
      "id": "czht4uy",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "2. REFLECTION OF LIGHT"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "honlius",
      "content": "Laws of Reflection:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "cmrt1pv",
      "content": "1. Angle of incidence = Angle of reflection."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "u9bzp63",
      "content": "2. Incident ray, reflected ray and normal lie in same plane."
    },
    {
      "id": "qmt2ctq",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "3. MIRROR FORMULA"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "e0ol47w",
      "content": "Mirror Formula: 1/f = 1/v + 1/u"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "x87vl46",
      "content": "Magnification (m) = -v/u"
    },
    {
      "id": "utyt4wq",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "4. TYPES OF MIRRORS"
    },
    {
      "id": "dx4ve2m",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "wnfktry",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Mirror Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Image Nature</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Example</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Plane Mirror</p>",
            "<p>Virtual, erect, same size</p>",
            "<p>Dressing mirror</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Concave Mirror</p>",
            "<p>Real/Virtual (depends on position)</p>",
            "<p>Shaving mirror</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Convex Mirror</p>",
            "<p>Virtual, diminished</p>",
            "<p>Rear-view mirror</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "6qhlzd6",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "5. REFRACTION OF LIGHT"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "joa76ds",
      "content": "Refraction is bending of light when it passes from one medium to another."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jgmpyvg",
      "content": "Snell’s Law: n = sin i / sin r"
    },
    {
      "id": "qrligub",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "6. REFRACTIVE INDEX"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "sj3jqkp",
      "content": "Refractive Index (n) = Speed of light in vacuum / Speed in medium"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "vobjr7x",
      "content": "n = c / v"
    },
    {
      "id": "602x1wm",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "7. LENS FORMULA"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "w5f4vd1",
      "content": "Lens Formula: 1/f = 1/v - 1/u"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "uvv5v1t",
      "content": "Magnification (m) = v/u"
    },
    {
      "id": "7qjicx6",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "8. TYPES OF LENSES"
    },
    {
      "id": "lhh4paj",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Lens Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Image Nature</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Example</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Convex Lens</p>",
            "<p>Real &amp; inverted (generally)</p>",
            "<p>Magnifying glass</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Concave Lens</p>",
            "<p>Virtual &amp; diminished</p>",
            "<p>Spectacles (myopia)</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b6x3qfj",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "9. POWER OF LENS"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "kdwy1gm",
      "content": "Power (P) = 1/f (f in metre)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "kl34q7l",
      "content": "Unit: Dioptre (D)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "h5l8ufq",
      "content": "Convex lens → Positive power"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "mc9epbe",
      "content": "Concave lens → Negative power"
    },
    {
      "id": "yghra0u",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "10. DISPERSION OF LIGHT"
    },
    {
      "id": "ym9am49",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": ""
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7lns5ts",
      "content": "Splitting of white light into seven colours (VIBGYOR)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "05nbog4",
      "content": "Caused by prism."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2orn9yj",
      "content": "Red has maximum wavelength; Violet has minimum wavelength."
    },
    {
      "id": "4vnmllg",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "11. TOTAL INTERNAL REFLECTION"
    },
    {
      "id": "sbnt906",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "m8flshj",
      "src": "/books/Precis/RRB GS/images/image_m8flshj.png"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ebznpc5",
      "content": "Occurs when light travels from denser to rarer medium."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9wz301n",
      "content": "Angle of incidence &gt; Critical angle."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ulcoo9k",
      "content": "Applications: Optical fibre."
    },
    {
      "id": "ml4lf65",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "12. DEFECTS OF VISION"
    },
    {
      "id": "dyf783z",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Defect</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Cause</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Correction</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Myopia</p>",
            "<p>Image forms before retina</p>",
            "<p>Concave lens</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Hypermetropia</p>",
            "<p>Image forms behind retina</p>",
            "<p>Convex lens</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Presbyopia</p>",
            "<p>Weak eye muscles</p>",
            "<p>Bifocal lens</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "wkkoew1",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "13. SIGN CONVENTION (CARTESIAN SIGN RULE)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "bhgnc3r",
      "content": "• All distances are measured from pole (mirror) or optical centre (lens)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ozsrv8z",
      "content": "• Distances measured in direction of incident light → Positive."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "pdipzff",
      "content": "• Distances measured opposite to incident light → Negative."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ruk986k",
      "content": "• Heights above principal axis → Positive."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "qe81bqf",
      "content": "• Heights below principal axis → Negative."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hk8djxc",
      "content": "Very common RRB numerical trap area."
    },
    {
      "id": "d5q26cu",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "14. IMPORTANT RAY DIAGRAM RULES (MIRRORS)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "l8o0vmk",
      "content": "• Ray parallel to principal axis → Passes through focus (concave)."
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Property",
        "Concave Lens",
        "Convex Lens"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Beam Effect",
          "Diverging",
          "Converging"
        ],
        [
          "Focal Length (f)",
          "Negative (-)",
          "Positive (+)"
        ],
        [
          "Power (P)",
          "Negative Dioptres",
          "Positive Dioptres"
        ],
        [
          "Vision Correction",
          "Myopia (Short-sightedness)",
          "Hypermetropia (Far-sightedness)"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "80nlg6u"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "aybn3kz",
      "content": "• Ray passing through focus → Becomes parallel to axis."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "czsg355",
      "content": "• Ray passing through centre of curvature → Retraces its path."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hihx3nr",
      "content": "• For convex mirror, reflected rays appear to diverge from focus."
    },
    {
      "id": "18rdgit",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "15. IMPORTANT RAY DIAGRAM RULES (LENSES)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0fnozbl",
      "content": "• Ray parallel to axis → Passes through focus (convex lens)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gn481m7",
      "content": "• Ray through optical centre → Goes straight without deviation."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ifd18wv",
      "content": "• Ray through focus → Emerges parallel to principal axis."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "kr3mqlj",
      "content": "Concave lens always forms virtual, erect and diminished image."
    },
    {
      "id": "l1djs62",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "16. REFRACTION THROUGH GLASS SLAB"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "sgqr97r",
      "content": "Emergent ray is parallel to incident ray."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "mt3if44",
      "content": "There is lateral displacement."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "p7bmmq2",
      "content": "No change in direction if slab surfaces are parallel."
    },
    {
      "id": "txyzgfr",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "17. CRITICAL ANGLE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xj530q3",
      "content": "Critical angle is the angle of incidence in denser medium for which angle of refraction is 90°."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "7heotag",
      "content": "Formula: sin C = 1/n"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3ir5tyu",
      "content": "If angle of incidence &gt; Critical angle → Total Internal Reflection occurs."
    },
    {
      "id": "ic37lv4",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "18. COMBINATION OF LENSES"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "aasudhz",
      "content": "For lenses in contact:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "uo02d46",
      "content": "1/F = 1/f1 + 1/f2"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "fgtt4p0",
      "content": "Power: P = P1 + P2"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "f4y3jbr",
      "content": "Frequently asked in RRB numerical problems."
    },
    {
      "id": "mzbxnvb",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "19. MAGNIFICATION (DETAILED)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "76ry22d",
      "content": "For mirrors: m = -v/u"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Pay strict attention to sign conventions: Focal length is negative for concave optical elements and positive for convex ones.",
        "Conditions for Total Internal Reflection (denser to rarer medium, i > critical angle) are repeatedly tested in RRB exams.",
        "Differentiate clearly between Mirror Formula (1/f = 1/v + 1/u) and Lens Formula (1/f = 1/v - 1/u)."
      ],
      "id": "hpxa726"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "1tt94fl",
      "content": "For lenses: m = v/u"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "o7vmri4",
      "content": "If m &gt; 1 → Image enlarged."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "arif0t1",
      "content": "If m &lt; 1 → Image diminished."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "i75bboc",
      "content": "If m is positive → Image is virtual and erect."
    },
    {
      "id": "m4dnybz",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "20. SCATTERING OF LIGHT"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "i8cgblz",
      "content": "Scattering is deviation of light by particles."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "qwpbwqp",
      "content": "Shorter wavelength scatters more (Violet/Blue)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6gshhq8",
      "content": "Reason for blue colour of sky."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "dtq2q4x",
      "content": "Sun appears red at sunrise/sunset due to scattering."
    },
    {
      "id": "i1wmjr9",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "21. ADVANCED RRB EXAM POINTS"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "zu6e40u",
      "content": "• Speed of light is maximum in vacuum."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wnmlysk",
      "content": "• Frequency of light does not change during refraction."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2jj9lq2",
      "content": "• Magnification is positive for virtual image."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "25twrc1",
      "content": "• Convex mirror always forms virtual image."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "26eceta",
      "content": "• Power of lens increases when focal length decreases."
    }
  ],
  "enriched": true
}